## ABSTRACT
We report gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) doped iron-based metal-organic frameworks (GIM) which displays near-infrared light (NIR)-enhanced cascade nanozyme against hypoxic tumors. Due to the strong protein adsorption-induced surface passivation, AuNPs suffer from the loss of glucose oxidase (GOx) activity. However, GIM could protect the GOx-like activity of AuNPs with the satisfactory shield capability. In addition, GIM exhibited excellent photothermal conversion ability and unique NIR light-enhanced GOx-like activity, which could efficiently increase the endogenous \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}\) production. Meanwhile, as the produced \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}\) is converted by GIM into \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) and highly toxic \(\mathsf{O H}\). Thus, GIM-catalyzed cascade reactions with NIR light irradiation not only offer the \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) but also promote the reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation at tumor sites. The produced \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) could be further applied to AuNPs catalytic oxidation of glucose and relieve hypoxic condition of tumor microenvironment (TME). As a proof-of-concept study, GIM demonstrates the admirable tumor ablation under NIR irradiation in vivo.
## KEYWORDS
nanozyme, metal-organic frameworks, gold nanoparticles, near-infrared light, reactive oxygen species
## 1 Introduction
Excessive reactive oxygen species (ROS) can cause cell damage by oxidizing amino acids of proteins and unsaturated fatty acids in lipids, irreversibly damaging to DNA. Therefore, manipulation of ROS levels by redox modulation has been proved to be an effective strategy for cancer treatment [1–6]. Among the ROS-mediated cancer therapies, chemo dynamic therapy (CDT) could selectively respond to the tumor microenvironment (TME) and prevent damage to normal tissues [7]. For example, hydroxyl radicals (·OH) induces tumor cell apoptosis through the intra-tumoral hydrogen peroxide \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)\) participated in the Fenton reaction and/or Fenton-like reaction. However, in view of the high concentration of glutathione which could consume ·OH and low concentration of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in the TME, CDT could not produce satisfactory therapeutic effects due to the insufficient ·OH.
Generally increasing the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) concentration of TME is regarded as a typical method responsible to improve the ·OH generation. Some chemicals such as ascorbic acid, \(\upbeta\) -lapachone and cinnamaldehyde were used to stimulate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation in cancer cells [8–10]. Meanwhile, glucose oxidase (GOx) could be served as an alternative method that have been widely utilized to increase the endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) sources in CDT [11–14]. The GOx catalyzes glucose into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in the presence of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), and then \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) could generate ·OH through the Fenton reaction and/or Fenton-like reaction. This cascade reaction employed
external GOx to enhance the CDT efficiency. However, the natural GOx expose some fatal flaws such as high cost in preparation and purification, relatively poor stability, short half-life in vivo, and harsh usage conditions, which will hamper its therapeutic application [15].
In the last decade, a wide variety of inorganic nanomaterials named "nanozyme" have been reported to exhibit specific natural enzyme-mimic catalytic properties [16–19]. Compared with natural enzymes, multiple advantages, such as low-cost, bulk-preparation, facile fabrication, tunable catalytic activities and robust stability against severe conditions, make nanozymes attractive in many fields, including biosensing, antibacterial applications, environmental monitoring and disease treatment [20–23]. Since gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) have been reported to possess the GOx-like activity [24–26], great efforts have been made to apply AuNPs-nanozyme in biological systems [27]. Despite the excellent GOx-like catalytic activity, enzyme-like activity of AuNPs cannot be directly applied for cancer treatment as the activity will lost in physiological conditions owing to strong protein adsorption [28].
MOFs possess a porosity characteristic with pervasive applications in catalysts, sensors, drug carriers, and imaging agents and so on [29–31]. In this work, we designed gold nanoparticles doped iron-based MOFs nanomaterial (GIM), which demonstrated near-infrared light (NIR) light-enhanced nanozyme activity to promote ROS generation by catalytic cascade reactions against hypoxic tumors (Schemes 1(b) and (c)). GIM could
consume glucose and reduce the glucose uptake at tumor sites due to the \(\mathrm{GOx}\)-like activity of AuNPs. This reaction will generate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and gluconic acid that enhances endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) level and the acidity in the tumor microenvironment, which is in favor of CDT. The GIM also can react with \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) by Fenton reaction. In addition, GIM can decompose \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) rapidly, which not only can relieve hypoxic condition of TME, but offer \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for AuNPs catalytic oxidation of glucose.
It is noteworthy that high activity is one of the prerequisites for nanozymes in vivo applications. GIM showed unique advantages to make the nanomaterial performing the desired activity for cancer therapy. On one hand, the MOFs could protect the enzyme-like activity of AuNPs from strong protein adsorption in biological fluids. The porous structure of MOFs provides many active sites and favors mass transport during the catalytic process. On the other hand, AuNPs highly integrated into GIM could generate local heat stimulation upon NIR light irradiation. It would greatly enhance the nanozyme activity of GIM and be beneficial to speed up the catalytic cascade reactions to produce more ROS, which induces cell apoptosis significantly and inhibition of tumor growth. Therefore, the well-defined GIM not only enhanced nanozyme catalytic cascade reactions with NIR light stimuli in the absence of the external \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) sources, but also could realize the modulation of nanozyme activity by changing the NIR light irradiation power density and time.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of GIM
Iron based MOFs (IM) was firstly synthesized and GIM with purple color was then obtained by in-situ reduction of \(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) in the presence of IM. For the synthesis of IM, \(0.58\,\mathrm{~g~}\) of \(\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(0.42\mathrm{~g~}\) of \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}\) were added into \(100~\mathrm{mL}\) of absolute ethyl alcohol. The reaction solution was incubated to reaction at \(50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{{h}}\) , and the produced IM was separated
by centrifuging at \(6{,}000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(10\;\mathrm{min}\) and washed five times with water. For the synthesis of GIM, \(3~\mathrm{mg}\) of IM and \(0.5~\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}\) solution \((28\;\mathrm{mM})\) were mixed into \(20~\mathrm{mL}\) of water. The mixture was stirred for \(5\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . Next, \(0.1\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) \(\left(100\;\mathrm{mM}\right)\) was quickly added to the mixture and stirred for \(3\,\mathrm{~h~}\) in the ice-water bath. The color of IM turned from orange to purple. After the reaction, the as-prepared GIM were separated by the centrifugation at \(6{,}000\,\mathrm{\rpm}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{\min}\) , washed and finally dispersed in DI water.
## 2.2 Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) measurements
For -OH detection, the buffer solution \(\mathrm{pH}\,5.0\) and 7.4, \(25\;\mathrm{mM}\) ) with DMPO \((50~\mathrm{mM})\) , IM \((0.1~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) \(\left(100\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\right)\) was prepared. After incubation for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) , EPR spectra were recorded.
## 2.3 Detection of \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) from IM
IM \((0.1\ \mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) \((0.1\ \mathrm{mM})\) and TMB \((0.5\ \mathrm{mM})\) were incubated in \(3\,\mathrm{\mL}\) of buffer solution while varying the \(\mathrm{pH}\) from 3 to 8 (3–6 acetate buffer and 7–8 phosphate buffer). The absorbance spectrum at \(625~\mathrm{nm}\) was recorded using a UV–vis spectrometer.
## 2.4 GOx-like activity of GIM
1,4-Dicarboxybenzene (PTA) with negligible fluorescence by itself can react with \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) generating 2-hydroxy terephthalic acid (TAOH) with unique fluorescence around \(425~\mathrm{nm}\) . GIM \((0.5\mathrm{~mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) , glucose \((5~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) and PTA \((2\ \mathrm{mM})\) were incubated in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of buffer \(\left(\mathrm{pH}\ 5.0\right)\) magnetically stirring for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . Then the spectra under the excitation wavelength of \(315\;\mathrm{nm}\) were tested using a fluorescence spectrometer.
## 2.5 Photothermal effect of GIM under NIR light irradiation
## 2.6 The GIM-catalyzed cascade reactions under NIR light irradiation
GIM \((0.5~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) , glucose \((5~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) and PTA \((2\ \mathrm{mM})\) were mixed by magnetically stirred in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) of buffer \(\left(\mathrm{pH}\,5.0\right)\) irradiated with \(808{\mathrm{-nm}}\) NIR laser (power density: \(2\ \mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) ) for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) . After that, the reaction solution was transferred into quartz cuvette and observed by a fluorescence spectrometer. To simulate hypoxic conditions, the above solutions were prepared by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) saturated water in the experiment.
## 2.7 GOx-like activity of GIM and AuNPs in BSA solution
AuNPs were synthesized as the same method as GIM just without addition of IM, and purified by ultrafiltration centrifugation at \(1,700g\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) . To test the GOx-like activities, the GIM and AuNPs were separately mixed with glucose \((50~\mathrm{mM})\) and incubated for \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) under constant stirring. The reaction product of gluconic acid was assayed by reaction with hydroxylamine and subsequent complex with \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) . In order to prevent the influence of AuNPs, the catalytic reaction solution was ultrafiltrated or centrifuged to remove the AuNPs or
GIM respectively after the catalytic reaction. Briefly, \(200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of solution 1 (5 mM EDTA and \(0.15\;\mathrm{mM}\) \(\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) in water) and \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of solution 2 (3 M \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) in water) were added to the catalytic reaction solution, and the mixture was allowed to react for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\). Finally, \(62\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of solution 3 (1 M HCl, \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}\), and \(0.25\;\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{CCl}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}\) in water) was added to the above reaction medium. The reaction was further incubated for \(5\ \mathrm{min}\) at room temperature before measurement of absorbance at \(505\,\mathrm{\nm}\) for formation of the reddish hydroxamate-Fe(III) complex. The GOx-like activities of GIM and AuNPs in a serious of diluted BSA solution were investigated by UV–vis spectra.
## 2.8 \(\mathbf{O}_{2}\) generation from GIM
\(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}(10\ \mathrm{mM})\) was added into \(40~\mathrm{mL}\) of deoxygenated water. GIM with different concentrations were added into the above solution. The concentrations of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in GIM solution (0, 10, 20, and \(50~\mathrm{mg\cdotmL^{-1}}\)) were recorded by a dissolved oxygen meter in real time.
## 2.9 Cell culture
The human lung cancer cell line A549 and human hepatocyte cell line LO2 were obtained from the Institute of Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences (Shanghai, China). They were all cultured in DMEM medium supplemented with \(10\%\) fetal bovine serum (FBS) and \(1\%\) penicillin-streptomycin in an incubator where the temperature was set at \(37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) humidified atmosphere. Hypoxia preconditioning was performed as cells cultured for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) at \(95\%\,\mathrm{N}_{2},2\%\,\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \(5\%\,{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\).
## 2.10 Determination of ROS production in vitro
For ROS detection, A549 cells were randomly seeded in 96-well plates \((2\times10^{4}\) cells per well) and then cultured for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) before further manipulation. Then A549 cells were incubated with different treatments (including the incubation of IM, GIM at different concentrations and laser irradiation times, etc.). After that, the treated cells were washed with PBS twice and incubated with DCFH-DA \((10~\upmu\mathrm{M})\) for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\). After the unloaded probe was removed with PBS, the fluorescence intensities of cells were observed by fluorescence microscopy. A549 cells were randomly seeded in 24-well plates \((2\,\times\,10^{4}\) cells per well) which treated as before for a flow cytometry assay.
## 2.11 Cytotoxicity assay in vitro
LO2 and A549 cells were seeded in 96-well plates \((2\times10^{4}\) cells per well) in DMEM (containing \(10\%\) FBS and \(1\%\) penicillin-streptomycin) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). Then, the A549 cells were incubated with different treatments for an additional \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). After that, the A549 cells were washed with PBS. Then, CCK-8 solution \((10\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\ /100\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) medium) was added to each well. After incubation for another \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\). \(\mathrm{OD}_{488}\) value (Abs.) with background subtraction at \(650~\mathrm{nm}\) was detected by a microplate photometer. Cell viability (\%) was obtained according to the following equation: Cell viability (\%) = (OD Treated/OD Control) \(\times\,100\%\). Where OD Control was obtained without nanoparticles, and OD Treated was in the presence of nanoparticles. A549 cells stained with Calcein-AM and PI as a protocol, followed by monitor with fluorescence microscope. YF488-Annexin V and PI Apoptosis Kit was applied as a protocol and the results were obtained by a flow cytometer.
## 2.12 Intracellular generation of \(\mathbf{O}_{2}\) under hypoxic condition
\([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dpp})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) was used as an indicator to test the intracellular production of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) by fluorescence microscope imaging. The
red fluorescence of \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dpp})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) under hypoxic condition was observed by fluorescence microscopy. A549 cells were seeded in 96-well plates in DMEM for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (\(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(95\%\) \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(2\%\) \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)). Then, \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dpp})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) medium solution \((5~\upmu\mathrm{M})\) was added to the cells for another \(4\,\mathrm{{h}}\). After that, the A549 cells were washed with PBS and further incubated with IM and GIM for \(8\,\mathrm{{h}}\), respectively. The fluorescence intensities of \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dpp})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) after different treatments under hypoxic condition were observed by fluorescence microscope.
## 2.13 Tumor xenografts
Animal procedures were performed according to the guidelines of the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee of the Nanjing Drum Tower Hospital, the Affiliated Hospital of Nanjing University Medical School. Cultured A549 cells were digested by trypsin-EDTA solution and then harvested by centrifugation (1,000 rpm for \(5\;\mathrm{min}\)). After being resuspended in sterile PBS solution, A549 cells (\(10^{8}\) cells per mouse) were subcutaneously implanted into about \(18\,\mathrm{g}\) of female Blab/c nude mice for further use.
## 2.14 Detection of tumor suppression in vivo
Tumor-bearing mice (tumor size of ca. \(80\;\mathrm{mm}^{3}\)) were randomly divided into five groups which were treated with PBS, IM (without NIR light), \(\mathrm{IM}+\mathrm{NIR}\) light, GIM (without NIR light), and \(\mathrm{GIM}+\mathrm{NIR}\) light (\(20~\upmu\mathrm{L},\,2~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\), \(n=5\)), respectively. The formulations above were adopted through intra-tumor injection every \(2\;\mathrm{d}\). \(808\mathrm{-nm}\) NIR laser (power density: \(1.0\,\mathrm{W}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), \(10\ \mathrm{min}\)) was applied as a vertical beam into the tumor sites. The administration was monitored for up to \(14\;\mathrm{d}\) and during that time. The body weights and tumor volumes were recorded for further analysis, and the animals were euthanized on day 14. The tumor volumes were calculated as follows: volume \(=\) (length \(\times\) width\(^2\))/2.
## 2.15 Biodistribution and metabolism of GIM in vivo
In the quantitative biodistribution analysis, A549 tumor-bearing mice were intra-tumor injected with GIM (\(20~\upmu\mathrm{L},\,2~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\)) and randomly divided into three groups (\(n=3\)). At \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) post administration, the mice were sacrificed. The tumor tissues, heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney were excised and weighted. For metabolism study, after intra-tumor administration with GIM, the fecal and renal excretions of the mice were harvested and weighed from the first day to the fourth day. The samples were digested with \(\mathrm{HNO}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) at \(90\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and the concentrations of Au ion in the samples were analyzed by inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS).
## 2.16 H&E, Ki67 antibody and HIF-1\(\alpha\) staining and TUNEL
For histology, mice's tumors and major organs from mice after different treatments were harvested. After fixing in \(4\%\) paraformaldehyde, the collected organs and tumors were embedded in paraffin, sectioned into about \(4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) thickness for hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), Ki67 antibody and HIF-1\(\alpha\) staining. Moreover, TUNEL Apoptosis Assay Kit was applied to determine tumor cell apoptosis, respectively. The histology was performed at the Nanjing Drum Tower Hospital. The samples were observed by the Invitrogen EVOS FL Auto Cell Imaging System (Thermo Fisher Scientific).
## 3 Results and discussion
Scheme 1(a). The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image (Fig. 1(a)) shows that iron-based MOFs (IM) exhibited the unique needle bundle-like structure with an average diameter and length around 60 and \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) respectively. Owing to the porous structure of the MOFs, the AuNPs \(\left(\sim10\ \mathrm{nm}\right)\) were grown both inside and outside of the IM (Fig. 1(b)), which demonstrated obvious GOx-like activity. Au nanozyme was chosen as GOx substitute on account of its advantages of easy preparation, excellent catalytic ability in a broad \(\mathrm{pH}\) and temperature range, good NIR light absorption and high stability compared with natural GOx. The morphology and composition of GIM could be evidenced by TEM and energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) analysis, which shows obvious signals for Au and Fe in the nanostructure (Fig. 1(c)). In addition, our Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR), X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns analysis, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption experiments confirmed the successful preparation of GIM (Fig. 1(d)–1(f) and Fig. S1 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. S1(a) in the ESM, the peak positions of Fe \(2{\mathrm{p}}_{3/2}\) and Fe \(2{\mathrm{p}}_{1/2}\) are 712.2 and \(725.6\;\mathrm{eV},\) respectively, which indicates Fe(III). The Fe \(2{\mathrm{p}}_{3/2}\) peak at \(716.6~\mathrm{eV}\) is attributed to Fe(II) as previously reported [32], which demonstrates the coexistence of Fe(II) and Fe(III) in the IM. There was no significant degradation for GIM observed in neutral and acid cell culture medium after incubation for \(48\,\mathrm{~h~}\) (Fig. S2 in the ESM), indicating a good stability of GIM.
## 3.2 Catalytic performance assessments of GIM
Detection of \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) from IM was investigated by catalyze \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and TMB to produce the blue product ox-TMB with absorption
Detection of \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) from IM was investigated by catalyze \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and TMB to produce the blue product ox-TMB with absorption at \(652\;\mathrm{nm}\). Figure S4(a) in the ESM shows that the optimal \(\mathrm{pH}\) is between 3 and 5, which indicates that the \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) generation from IM is a \(\mathrm{pH}\)-dependent manner. As illustrated by EPR spectra (Fig. S4(b) in the ESM), IM could generate \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) under a mild acidic environment. Therefore, the Fenton reaction selectively occurred at the tumor site, rather than normal tissue at physiological \(\mathrm{pH}\). Thereby, the produced \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) would only damage the tumor tissue without obvious side effect on normal tissue.
The construct GIM can effectively protect the GOx-like activities of AuNPs to reduce the suppression effect. It has been reported that AuNPs could suffer from surface passivation due to the strong protein adsorption in biological fluids [29], which could block the surface active-site of AuNPs and impact further catalysis. To confirm GIM could protect the GOx-like activity of AuNPs, the GOx-like activities of AuNPs and GIM were carefully investigated in the presence of bovine serum albumin (BSA) (Fig. S3 in the ESM). It can be seen that GIM shows the obvious GOx-like activity compared to AuNPs in the presence of BSA. As demonstrated in Fig. 2(a), the GOx-like activities of GIM and AuNPs both reduced in the presence of BSA. Because some AuNPs located in the outside of GIM, the part of these AuNPs without MOFs protection could suffer from protein absorption. Nevertheless, the suppression on GOx-like activity of AuNPs was more obvious than that of GIM in BSA solution. Even in the presence of \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) BSA solution, the catalytic activity of AuNPs nearly disappeared, while GIM still exhibited the significant GOx-like activity. The results suggested that the AuNPs grown inside the GIM and partial encapsulated in the GIM can maintain the GOx-like activity
by shielding AuNPs from the protein absorption. Additionally, GIM with NIR light irradiation showed much higher catalytic activity than that without irradiation in the presence of protein. Whereas AuNPs that have lost GOx-like activity due to BSA absorption and do not show any catalytic activity even irradiated by NIR light. Therefore, owing to the characteristic protection and NIR light enhanced-nanozyme property, GIM could compromise the inhibitory activity caused by the high protein concentration absorption.
The terephthalic acid (TA) was utilized to test ·OH as it could react with ·OH to form a strong fluorescent 2-hydroxy terephthalic acid (TAOH) with the maximum emission at \(425~\mathrm{nm}\) [32]. As shown in Fig. S4(c) in the ESM, there was a fluorescence peak for GIM in the presence of glucose, demonstrating that its capability to generate ·OH by the catalytic cascade reactions. Under NIR light irradiation, we found that the GIM would produce more ·OH. Owing to the excellent photothermal conversion ability of GIM, the solution temperature was increased upon NIR light exposure (Fig. 2(b)). The rising temperature could enhance the GOx-like activity of GIM and accelerate the catalytic cascade reaction, which facilitates the ·OH production by providing more \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for subsequent Fenton reaction (Fig. 2(c)). In addition, GIM can also generate \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). (Fig. S4(d) in the ESM) which relieves the hypoxia of the tumor. As can been seen in Fig. S4(e) in the ESM, the fluorescence intensity increased with increasing NIR light irradiation power density under the simulated hypoxia condition. It means GIM could also improve the ROS generation under NIR light irradiation in the hypoxia of tumor site. It can be seen that GIM exhibited no GOx-like activity in absence of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Therefore, such results manifested that GIM not only relieves the hypoxia of the TME, but offers the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to take part in the glucose oxidation reaction, which favors the ROS generation.
Intracellular ROS-generated capacity of GIM was evaluated in A549 (lung adenocarcinoma cell line) cells by using of 2,7-dichlorfluorescein diacetate (DCFH-DA) probe. Compared
with IM groups (with and without light irradiation) and GIM group without light irradiation, A549 cells incubated with GIM showed strong green fluorescence under NIR light irradiation. As shown in Figs. S5 and S6 in the ESM, A549 cells treated with GIM displayed the fluorescence signals increased as power density and incubation time increased. The same results can be found in our flow cytometry assays (Fig. 2(d)), which can be conclude that GIM exhibits NIR light-enhanced capacity that improves nanozyme-catalyzed cascade reaction effect and generates much more ROS than other control groups.
GIM with irradiation group also produced more ROS than other control groups under hypoxic conditions (Figs. 2(e)–2(g)). The \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) generation properties of GIM in cancer cells were examined by using of \([\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{dpp})_{3}]\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\) as \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) probe. Under hypoxic conditions, the red fluorescence in cancer cells was quenched by the generated \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) with GIM or IM treatment (Fig. S7 in the ESM), indicating both of them could reduce hypoxia of TME by converting intracellular \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\). However, GIM with NIR light irradiation showed maximum alleviating of hypoxia, which was further proved by the immunohistochemistry staining of hypoxia-inducible factor-1α (HIF-1α) protein in tumor tissues. HIF-1α fluorescence intensity observed was obviously reduced after addition of GIM (Figs. 2(h)–2(k)). Therefore, GIM with NIR light irradiation could efficiently utilize \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and catalyze the glucose to produce more \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), hence GIM generate more ROS than other groups under hypoxic condition.
## 3.3 NIR light-enhanced apoptosis of A549 cells in vitro
To study of the apoptosis intuitively, A549 cells treated with GIM under different conditions were stained with calcein-AM (green fluorescence) and propidium iodide (PI) (red fluorescence). The results could be investigated by fluorescence microscope. As shown in Fig. 3(a), there were more cancer cells dead accompanied with NIR light irradiation when compared to GIM group without NIR light irradiation. It indicated that the NIR
light irradiation can significantly promote efficient generation of ROS, which modulates intracellular oxidative stress to induce cell death.
The biocompatibility of GIM was assessed by cytotoxicity test against LO2 cells using cell counting kit 8 (CCK-8) assays. As shown in Fig. S8 in the ESM, cell viability remained above \(90\%\) at test concentrations after \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) incubation, indicating GIM exhibited no apparent cytotoxic effect and good biocompatibility. We further evaluated the in vitro cytotoxicity of GIM using CCK-8 assays. GIM group with NIR light irradiation showed a lower viability than GIM group without light irradiation and IM groups (with and without light irradiation) in both normoxia and hypoxia condition (Fig. S9 in the ESM). Furthermore, the cell viability decreased with the increase of the GIM concentration under NIR light irradiation (Figs. 3(b) and 3(c)). By contrast, NIR light irradiation has a negligible influence on the viability of A549 cells for IM group. As a result, it identified that GIM with NIR light irradiation could increase the ROS generation to induce apoptosis of cancer cell in both normoxia and hypoxia. These results were consistent with the
LIVE (green)/DEAD (red) staining assays and the flow cytometry analysis (Fig. 3(d)).
## 3.4 The antitumor effects of GIM in vivo
As a proof of concept study, the antitumor efficacy of GIM in vivo was evaluated in the xenograft-bearing mice after different treatments via direct intra-tumoral injections (Fig. 4). Tumor-bear mice were randomly divided into five groups, including control, IM, \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{IM}+\mathrm{NIR}\) light radiation, GIM and \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{GIM}+\mathrm{NIR}\) light radiation. The tumor sizes of each group were measured by a caliper every other day. The tumor growth curves showed that GIM with NIR light irradiation exhibited admirable antitumor outcome as compared to IM, \(\scriptstyle\mathrm{IM}+\mathrm{NIR}\) light radiation and GIM (Figs. 4(a)–4(c) and Fig. S10 in the ESM). The tumor tissues were retrieved and processed for hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase dUTP nick-end labeling (TUNEL) and Ki67 antibody analysis. GIM with NIR light irradiation could substantially reduce the number of tumor cells and increase the population of apoptotic cells than other groups (Fig. 4(e)), indicating the capacity of GIM to
inhibit tumor growth. Furthermore, the blood glucose levels of mice after the injection with GIM (intravenous injection and intra-tumoral injection) were also recorded within \(8\;\mathrm{h}\). As illustrated in Fig. S11(a) in the ESM, the value of blood glucose exhibited a moderate and transient decrease around 1-h post-injection, while spontaneously recovered in \(1\;\mathrm{h}\). There was no pathoglycemia during the therapeutic period. The Au contents in solubilized tissues were measured by ICP-MS. After intra-tumoral injection, the biodistribution analysis of GIM after \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) was studied. As illustrated in Fig. S11(b) in the ESM, the GIM were primarily accumulated in tumor. Moreover, the metabolism of GIM in vivo was also studied by monitored the Au concentrations of excrement. The time-dependent excretion curve suggested that the clearance of GIM was possibly through both fecal and renal excretions. The excreted concentrations of Au were steadily decreased over time (Fig. S11(c) in the ESM). In addition, it demonstrated negligible weight fluctuations, suggesting that the GIM did not induce adverse effects (Fig. 4(d)). H&E stained tissue sections display no obvious pathological change in the major organs of mice as compared with untreated group (Fig. S12 in the ESM). These in vivo results indicated that GIM could be an ideal candidate for further clinical applications.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we developed gold nanoparticles doped MOFs as NIR light-enhanced cascade nanozyme against hypoxic tumors. The construct GIM could protect the GOx-like activity of Au nanozyme. With the GOx-like activities, GIM can efficiently catalyze the oxidation of glucose to increase the endogenous
\(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) production, simultaneously converting intracellular \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) into \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) by Fenton reaction, which not only alleviates hypoxia of TME, but also be beneficial to continuous catalytic cascade reactions. Under the stimulation of NIR light irradiation, GIM-catalyzed cascade reactions were significantly improved, which promote the ROS generation to inhibit tumor growth. This strategy could be broadly used to engineer other nanozymes with the introduction of an external energy field in composite to promote the tumor catalytic therapy.
